<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Francis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/2025">2025</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jorge Mario Bergoglio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Francis, born Jorge Mario Bergoglio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> who served as Archbishop of Buenos Aires from 1998 and was elected pope on 13 March 2013. He was the first pope from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the first Jesuit pope, and his pontificate stressed care for the poor, migrants and the environment. He died on 21 April 2025.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-francis.jpg" alt="Pope Francis" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeon Han, Korea.net · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Francis_Korea_Haemi_Castle_19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jorge Mario Bergoglio was born on 17 December 1936 in Flores, a neighbourhood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, the eldest of five children. His father, Mario José Bergoglio, was an accountant and an Italian immigrant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>; his mother, Regina María Sívori, was a housewife born in Buenos Aires to a family of northern Italian origin. The family left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1929 to escape the fascist rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>. At 21, after life-threatening pneumonia and three cysts, he had part of a lung removed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Bergoglio entered the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as a novice on 11 March 1958, after three years at the archdiocesan seminary in Villa Devoto, and studied the humanities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. He took perpetual vows on 12 March 1960 and taught literature and psychology in Santa Fe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. He was ordained in 1969 and was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> provincial superior in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> from 1973 to 1979. In 1976 the Argentine Navy kidnapped two Jesuit priests, Orlando Yorio and Franz Jalics, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirty-war">Dirty War</a>. Bergoglio is widely reported to have failed to protect them; a criminal complaint filed in 2005 was dismissed, but the debate over his conduct has continued.</p><p>He became archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on the death of his predecessor, Quarracino, on 28 February 1998, and was created a cardinal in 2001 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a>. He doubled the number of priests working in the city's shantytowns, which earned him the name villero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and put the archdiocese's finances on a stricter footing. After the resignation of Pope Benedict XVI, the conclave elected him pope on 13 March 2013. He took the name Francis in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi">Francis of Assisi</a>, and was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> pope, the first from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and the first from the Southern Hemisphere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As pope Francis called for decentralised, synodal governance, made women full members of dicasteries of the Roman Curia and convened the Synod on Synodality. He was a behind-the-scenes broker, with the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, of the restoration of diplomatic relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> announced on 17 December 2014.</p><p>His last public appearance was on Easter Sunday, 20 April 2025. He died on Easter Monday, 21 April 2025, aged 88, of a cerebral stroke. His funeral took place on 26 April, and on 8 May the conclave elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiv">Robert Francis Prevost</a> as Pope Leo XIV. Francis had appointed more than 80 per cent of the cardinals who elected his successor, and he formalised the Church's opposition to the death penalty in all cases.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Francis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2013 the Archbishop of Buenos Aires, <b>Jorge Mario Bergoglio</b>, became Pope Francis, the first pope from the Americas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The murder of Archbishop Óscar Romero while saying mass on 24 March 1980 made him a worldwide symbol of liberation theology and of the defence of the poor; he was canonised by <b>Pope Francis</b> in 2018.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Francis</b> visited the site in March 2021.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Francis</b>, the first Latin American pope, drew from liberationist ideas, particularly in his advocacy for economic justice and environmental stewardship, as seen in his encyclical Laudato Si'.</blockquote><a href="/movement/liberation-theology">Liberation theology · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Critics include economists Joseph Stiglitz, Amartya Sen, Ha-Joon Chang and Thomas Piketty, the linguist Noam Chomsky, the geographer David Harvey and <b>Pope Francis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/neoliberalism">Neoliberalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2013 the theologian Leonardo Boff described <b>Pope Francis</b> as a Peronist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/peronism">Peronism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Flores, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in Domus Sanctae Marthae, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.63,-58.47&amp;year=1993" aria-label="Flores on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3065 3417 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Francis"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3515 3687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3515 3687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.63,-58.47&amp;year=1993">What was here in 1993? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
